  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1039 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nQUIET THROUGH FRIDAY EVENING\n\nThe ridge to our west is in the process of breaking down this \nevening, with increasing upper level moisture in the form of mid to \nhigh level clouds spilling in from the northwest. Aside from the \nclouds, seasonably mild temperatures and relatively quiet conditions \nwill continue through the day Friday.\n\nSNOWY INTO THE WEEKEND\n\nThe first in a series of shortwaves in the northwesterly flow \nreaches eastern Utah and western Colorado Friday evening. Light snow \nshowers are expected to begin along the Northern Divide and spread \nsouth through the night. The strongest in this series of waves \narrives Saturday morning, with the highest snowfall rates expected \nbetween 5AM and 5PM Saturday. This will impact morning and evening \ncommutes, as well as any post-Thanksgiving Holiday traffic over the \nmountain passes. A Winter Storm Warning has been issued for the Park \nand Elkhead mountains, and Winter Weather Advisories remain in \neffect for the Central Divide mountains and Flat Tops, all from 5PM \nFriday evening to 5AM Sunday morning. For the Park and Elkhead \nmountains, 10-14 inches of snow are expected, with locally higher \namounts possible. For the Central Divide Mountains and the Flat \nTops, snow totals of 4-8 inches are expected, with locally higher \namounts possible. This system will also bring strong northwesterly \nwinds capable of gusting to 45 mph. As a result, blowing and \ndrifting snow will also be a concern. If planning on travelling at \nany point on Saturday, be prepared for difficult winter driving \nconditions, with blowing snow reducing visibility and slick, messy \nroad conditions.\n\nSUNDAY AND BEYOND\n\nSnow showers will taper off Sunday morning, with only a few \norographically driven showers lingering along the Park Range under \ndrying northwesterly flow. A transient ridge builds in Sunday into \nMonday, bringing quieter conditions seasonably mild temperatures. \nThis ridge flattens through the day Monday, with flow shifting from \nnorthwesterly to more zonal in response to a wave moving into the \nPacific Northwest. Model confidence with the impact of this first \nwave remains low, however it is possible that increasing moisture \nand favorable orographics will result in some light snow showers \nalong the Divide. Blended guidance has reasonably high probabilities \n(50-70%) of an inch or two of snow with this first wave. Looking \ntowards the middle of the week, there is potential for a stronger, \nmore southerly system to impact the Four Corners and southern \nmountains. Model guidance remains in relatively poor agreement, with \nsignificant spread. The southerly track does increase potential for \natmospheric river moisture to accompany this system. However, the \nexact track of this system can make the difference between a solid \nmountain snowfall and seeing no snow at all. We shall have to wait \nand see how the forecast for this system develops over the coming \ndays.",
